You have reviewed this system eight times. Round 7: conditional approval of the proportionality reset pending four design changes. Round 8: you verified the four changes, passed the claims/benchmark corrections and Decision E's honesty, and left exactly four remaining items — three design changes and one wording change — stating: "Once those are added, I would regard the remaining work — VM escape attempts, credential searches, live-record migration, exact-version binding, branch-protection attacks, spend races, hostile voice tests, and verified removal of the old paths — as build-phase proof rather than further architectural debate."

All four are now in the document:

1. OWNER-CONTROLLED, CONTINUOUSLY VALID ADMISSION. Only the authenticated owner can create, change, or revoke admissions, the allowlist, connected-tool approvals, or the EMR refusal; these records live outside anything an agent can edit. Admission does not survive drift: the broker re-checks live repository state (automation, webhooks, integrations, publishing settings) against the recorded proof before each action and refuses on mismatch; a proposed change that would itself add deployment/publishing/privileged-automation behavior cancels the repo's admission until the owner re-admits. Repos are identified by GitHub's permanent repository ID. The "owner's tap = manual deploy step" exception now only counts where the tap physically starts the separate deployment and cannot rescue a repo where merge itself deploys.

2. GAUNTLET BEFORE THE FIRST PUSH. The sequencing conflict you found is resolved in the direction round 7 required: review happens inside the harness on the agent's isolated clone BEFORE the first push; there is no pre-review push path at all; no repository automation ever runs unreviewed agent code; the verdict binds to the exact version that is then pushed and merged.

3. THE OPERATION FILTER. The document now states GitHub's permission reality plainly (contents-write suffices to create releases and trigger certain automation, per GitHub's docs you cited), abandons the claim that token scoping alone delivers the prohibition, and places the broker behind an independently enforced operation filter — a separate component under a separate identity the broker cannot alter, permitting only push-branch / open-PR / merge-PR on allowlisted repo IDs and blocking release, tag, settings, and dispatch calls — designed to hold even if the broker is fooled or compromised, and attacked as part of the build (new acceptance cases 4b/4c/4d).

4. WORDING. "Real money" became "spending beyond the fixed limits"; the benchmark no longer says the autonomy risk lands "almost entirely" on merges and spend — it now names continuous audio exposure, provider disclosure, and queue flooding alongside them.

